<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Taulantii</h1><p class="st-meta">450 BCE – 230 BCE (approx.) · capital Epidamnos</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Illyrian kingdom around Epidamnus on the Albanian coast, whose king Glaukias fought Alexander the Great and raised the young Pyrrhus.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Taulantii held the coastal plain around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durres">Epidamnus</a> (Durrës) from at least the 5th century BCE; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thucydides">Thucydides</a> records them in the quarrel over Epidamnus that helped spark the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnesian-war">Peloponnesian War</a>. By the 4th century BCE they were ruled by kings.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Their kings controlled the hinterland of the Greek colonies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durres">Epidamnus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollonia-illyria">Apollonia</a>, trading with them and at times dominating them. In 335 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glaukias">Glaukias</a> joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleitus-of-illyria">Cleitus of Illyria</a> against Alexander at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-pelium">Pelion</a>. Glaukias later sheltered the infant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrrhus-of-epirus">Pyrrhus of Epirus</a> and in 312 BCE seized Epidamnus.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glaukias">Glaukias</a> the Taulantian kingdom faded from view. By the later 3rd century BCE their lands lay within the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/illyrian-kingdom">Illyrian Kingdom</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agron">Agron</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teuta">Teuta</a>, and after 229 BCE much of the coast came under Roman protection.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 450 BCE – 230 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdo